course: dictionary
lesson: 13778
title: Words that mean too much to bear
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you that something has gone past a limit. Intolerable is one of them. When something is intolerable, it is too bad, too painful or too annoying to put up with. «The noise from the building site was intolerable, so Mia covered her ears.» The noise was so loud that Mia could not bear it, so intolerable must mean too much to put up with.
explain: Look at the words around it to check. Ask yourself: what was too much? In the sentence, the noise was the problem, and covering her ears was what Mia did about it. Then swap in your meaning, «too much to put up with», and see if the sentence still makes sense. It does.
explain: In school rules or in the news, intolerable means something different. «Bullying is intolerable, and our school will not allow it.» Here it means that the behaviour will not be accepted at all. The sentence tells you a line has been drawn.
fact: Intolerable is an adjective. It describes a thing, such as a noise, a heat or a pain.
fact: «Intolerable» means too bad, painful or annoying to put up with.
fact: «Intolerably» is the adverb form, as in «The sofa was intolerably lumpy»: lumpy to the point of being unbearable.
fact: In formal writing, «intolerable behaviour» means behaviour that will not be accepted at all.
fact: When you meet intolerable, look for the limit: something has gone too far for someone to bear it.
exercise: «The noise from the building site was intolerable.» What does intolerable mean here? | Full of music | Quiet and calm | *Too loud and annoying to put up with | Nice and friendly
exercise: «Rudeness to new pupils is intolerable in our school.» What does intolerable suggest? | *It will not be accepted at all | It only happens on Fridays | It is the best way to make friends | It is a fun game to play at break
exercise: «The pain in his arm was intolerable, so he went to the nurse.» Find and copy the word that tells you the pain was too much to bear. | *intolerable | went | nurse | arm
test: «The teacher said the mess in the hall was intolerable.» What is the best meaning? | *Too bad to be allowed | Hidden under the table | Tidy and clean | Very small
test: «The sofa was intolerably lumpy, so Sam stood up.» What does intolerably mean? | A little bit | *So uncomfortable that it could not be borne | Quietly | Happily
exam: Dani says intolerable means a bit annoying. Is she right? | No, it means very happy | Yes, a little annoying is the same thing | Yes, it means very quiet | *No, it means too bad to put up with, not just a bit annoying
